## What not to do

- Don't commit `.env` files, cloud account IDs, or API keys.
- Name one library per job, and the condition under which that choice may
  be revisited. If a task seems to need a new library or framework,
  surface the trade-off before adding it rather than after.
- TODO: this repo's own bright lines (content restraint, forbidden
  dependencies, anything a mistake here would be expensive for).

## Contributing workflow

- Branch and open a PR for every change, even solo work and even a
  one-line fix — never commit directly to the default branch.
- Worktree by default. Branch in the main checkout only when the change is
  a single commit, touches nothing that renders, no other session has a
  worktree open, and no long-running job you would not want to interrupt
  is in flight; escalate to a worktree on any doubt.
- Run `git worktree list` before creating one, and continue in the
  worktree that already exists rather than opening a second for the same
  branch. Name its directory after the branch with slashes flattened to
  dashes, give each worktree its own install and its own dev-server port,
  and seed the gitignored state it needs by symlinking shared fixtures,
  never by copying them.
- Ship the README, CLAUDE.md, and changelog edit a change requires in the
  same PR, not as a follow-up.
- Deploy is manual: a merged PR is not a production update. TODO: name
  this repo's deploy command.
- Git state is shared across sessions — check the current branch
  immediately before every commit and push.
- Preview a rendered surface yourself before asking for approval, state
  the scope of what's being approved, and never self-approve; escalate
  rather than assume on a subjective call, an irreversible action, or
  anything crossing this repo's stated bright line.
- Include a surface the change should not have touched in that preview:
  an unaffected page is how you tell a defect you introduced from one
  that was already there. Preview is skippable only for pure docs edits,
  script-only changes that never run in a browser, dependency bumps that
  pass build and tests, and config with no visible surface; when in
  doubt, preview.

